# socraticode-health.install — this hook's install-hook.sh arguments.
#
# Read by .skills/doctor.sh (#224) as the repair line for a health hook that is
# on disk with no SessionStart registration. This is the hook #224 was filed
# for: it is silent when clean by design, so "installed, unregistered, never
# runs" and "installed, registered, nothing to report" produce byte-identical
# observable behaviour. The repo that most needs the check is the one that
# cannot tell it stopped.
#
# Format: the first line that is neither blank nor a comment is the argument
# list, appended to a resolved `bash <vendor>/…/install-hook.sh`.
#
# It transcribes Step C of ../references/code-exploration-policy.md, and
# tests/structural/test_doctor_hook_registration.py compares the two.
#
# The marker is deliberately distinct from the reminder hook's: markers are
# per-hook precisely so that one hook's dedupe-strip cannot evict the other's
# entry from the SessionStart array they share.
#
# --timeout 120 is this hook's constant, and it is the one hook in the cohort
# for which an implicit ceiling is worst (#259). It shells out to mcp-driver.mjs,
# which launches the server via `npx -y socraticode` — 4.0s warm on
# CannObserv/cli, a package download cold — and it stamps its once-per-UTC-day
# lock BEFORE doing the work, deliberately, so a transient Docker restart does
# not re-run and re-log on every same-day session. A timeout kill therefore
# consumes the day's attempt and reports nothing, and the lock guarantees no
# retry until tomorrow: the silent-when-clean hook becomes silent-when-killed,
# which is the ambiguity #179 is about. 120 matches skills-submodule-update.sh,
# the other network-touching hook, and sits above the 60s ceiling this hook
# already exports to the driver (HEALTH_TIMEOUT_MS) so the inner bound is the
# one that fires.
--hook socraticode-health.sh --skill init-socraticode --marker socraticode-health --copy-fallback --timeout 120
